KNOWNBUG (real libstdc++ <tuple>).  std::make_tuple(1, 2.0, 'a') then
std::get<0> reads a wrong value.  Narrowed (2026-07-10): a TWO-element tuple is
fine end-to-end (both `std::tuple<A,B> t(...)` construction and
`std::make_tuple(a,b)` with `get<0>`), but a THREE-element tuple fails --
`std::make_tuple(a,b,c)` yields a wrong `get<0>`, and a manual
`std::tuple<A,B,C> t(a,b,c)` fails constructor resolution ("found no match for
symbol 'tuple'").  A hand-written faithful minimal (a recursively-inheriting
`_Tuple_impl` with a distinct-named element pack + a `get`-style
`__get_helper<I>` deducing the base) works at all arities, so the residual is
specific to libstdc++'s tuple CONSTRUCTOR machinery at >=3 elements (the
_TupleConstraints-guarded variadic constructor / element storage), not the
recursive-inheritance get<> deduction (that derived-to-base deduction, incl. a
non-empty trailing pack, is fixed -- see
cpp11_derived_to_base_variadic_deduction).  Next: cvise the preprocessed
3-element case to isolate the constructor-machinery defect.
